WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.920 align:start position:19%
Your brain is lying to you right now,
and you will never catch it in the act.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:12.760 align:start position:19%
Tonight, seven tricks your own mind
plays on you daily.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:17.280 align:start position:19%
Quick question.
Have you ever been totally sure about

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:23.040 align:start position:19%
something?
Then learned you were completely wrong.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:28.080 align:start position:19%
Hold that moment. It was never your
fault. It was your wiring.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:32.680 align:start position:19%
Psychologists have mapped these glitches
for decades. They are called cognitive

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:40.320 align:start position:19%
biases.
Not flaws of stupid people. Doctors,

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:46.640 align:start position:19%
judges, and CEOs fall for them daily.
A brilliant friend of mine got bent by

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:52.240 align:start position:19%
these tricks. Number seven is the one
nobody talks about. Trick one,

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:56.440 align:start position:19%
confirmation bias, running in your head
right now.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:00.760 align:start position:19%
Your brain is no detective. It is a
lawyer defending a verdict already

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:07.840 align:start position:19%
reached.
Decide something is true, and your mind

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:13.080 align:start position:19%
filters reality. Agreement highlighted,
disagreement deleted.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:17.880 align:start position:19%
This is why arguments go nowhere.
Both sides finish collecting proof

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:23.680 align:start position:19%
before the talk started.
Two people watch one video and reach

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:29.840 align:start position:19%
opposite conclusions. The video did not
change. The bias did.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:35.240 align:start position:19%
I have done this myself, defended a diet
that clearly failed, ignoring my own

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:41.240 align:start position:19%
doctor's studies.
It feels like thinking, like research,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:45.760 align:start position:19%
but it is your brain admiring its own
reflection.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:51.600 align:start position:19%
What evidence would change my mind?
If nothing could, that is a bias, not a

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:57.840 align:start position:19%
belief.
Now the trick that empties bank accounts

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:04.680 align:start position:19%
and traps people in miserable jobs.
Trick two, the sunk cost fallacy.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:10.280 align:start position:19%
You paid too much to quit, so you keep
paying.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:16.600 align:start position:19%
10 minutes into an awful movie, you stay
because leaving would waste the ticket.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:22.000 align:start position:19%
People stay in dead relationships for
years

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:27.880 align:start position:19%
as if one more year reforms the rest.
Executives pour millions into failing

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:32.480 align:start position:19%
projects because admitting loss feels
worse than losing more.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:37.520 align:start position:19%
Quitting feels like failure.
Your ego prefers losing more to

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:43.440 align:start position:19%
admitting it lost.
This one hit close to home. I once spent

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:47.920 align:start position:19%
two years building something nobody
wanted.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:53.080 align:start position:19%
Casinos run on this. Every gambler
thinks, "I cannot leave now. I have

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:56.880 align:start position:19%
already lost so much."
Would you still choose this starting

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:03.519 align:start position:19%
fresh?
If not, the past is an excuse.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:10.120 align:start position:19%
The past is a sunk cost.
The future is still negotiable.

00:03:07.239 --> 00:03:17.440 align:start position:19%
Trick three hijacks your sense of value
itself before anyone even names a price.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:22.600 align:start position:19%
Trick three, anchoring.
The first number you hear becomes the

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:28.080 align:start position:19%
reference for everything after it.
Marked down from 1,000 to 200, any

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:33.000 align:start position:19%
jacket feels like a steal, even one
worth 150.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.800 align:start position:19%
In every negotiation, whoever names the
first number wins.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:42.040 align:start position:19%
It bends the whole conversation around
itself.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:46.920 align:start position:19%
Job interviews run on this. The first
offer sets the ceiling. Counter offers

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:53.560 align:start position:19%
just orbit their anchor.
Even a random wheel spin changes what

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:59.240 align:start position:19%
people pay. Your brain anchors to noise.
Retailers plant anchors daily. The

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:03.080 align:start position:19%
crossed out price was never real. It
exists to make the real price feel

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:10.080 align:start position:19%
small.
Set your number before seeing theirs.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:16.720 align:start position:19%
Bring your own anchor or swim in theirs.
Whoever names the first number controls

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:24.080 align:start position:19%
the table. Remember that.
Trick four, losing 100 hurts more than

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:28.240 align:start position:19%
winning 100 feels good.
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Yobi. Welcome to the Dark Mind

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:34.880 align:start position:19%
Files.
&gt;&gt; Trick four, loss aversion. Losses feel

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.960 align:start position:19%
twice as painful as equal gains feel
pleasant.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:43.680 align:start position:19%
Find 100,
smile for an hour.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:51.280 align:start position:19%
Lose 100, think about it for a week.
Free trials run on this brutally well.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:57.000 align:start position:19%
Once the trial ends, canceling feels
like losing something you owned.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:02.000 align:start position:19%
Past halfway, the next three run your
social feed.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:07.880 align:start position:19%
Brain workout, hit subscribe. Dark Mind
Files daily.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:12.280 align:start position:19%
FOMO is loss aversion in a party mask.
Your brain hears, "Act now or lose

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:17.960 align:start position:19%
forever."
Investors panic sell at the bottom, then

00:05:14.919 --> 00:05:23.680 align:start position:19%
buy back at the top. Loss aversion
charging a fee every time.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:28.280 align:start position:19%
Your closet proves it. You keep things
you never use because tossing them feels

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:33.480 align:start position:19%
like losing.
Nothing lost, only changed. You're not

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:38.200 align:start position:19%
losing a subscription, you're freeing
money.

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:42.240 align:start position:19%
Your brain treats losses like threats.
You are not in danger,

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:48.280 align:start position:19%
just uncomfortable.
Discomfort passes.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:53.080 align:start position:19%
Trick five decides what scares you and
what you argue about.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:59.800 align:start position:19%
It starts with your newsfeed.
Trick five, the availability heuristic.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:04.720 align:start position:19%
Your brain judges likelihood by ease of
recall.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:09.520 align:start position:19%
Your memory is not a database. It is a
highlight reel. Drama gets front row

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:15.520 align:start position:19%
seats.
You fear planes more than cars, though

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:20.000 align:start position:19%
the airport drive is deadlier. Memorable
is not likely.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:24.960 align:start position:19%
You dread sharks, but vending machines
kill more people. One makes movies, the

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:31.640 align:start position:19%
other makes nothing.
The news manufactures availability.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:36.880 align:start position:19%
One rare tragedy replayed for a week
feels like a daily event.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:43.840 align:start position:19%
Smart people refuse to fly after one
news cycle, then text while driving.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:50.200 align:start position:19%
Fear follows footage, not facts.
Lotteries sell availability. You see the

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:54.840 align:start position:19%
one TV winner, never the 40 million
losers.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:59.880 align:start position:19%
The antidote is boring. Ask what the
numbers say.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:04.040 align:start position:19%
Boring numbers beat dramatic memories
every time.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:10.760 align:start position:19%
Memorable is not the same as likely.
Tattoo that on the inside of your skull.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:15.840 align:start position:19%
Trick six kept your ancestors alive.
Today, it sells tickets, trends, and

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:21.720 align:start position:19%
political movements.
Trick six, the bandwagon effect.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:26.000 align:start position:19%
Everyone is doing it, so your brain
reads safety.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:30.840 align:start position:19%
You join the crowded restaurant, not for
the food. 40 strangers cannot be wrong.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:34.520 align:start position:19%
They can.
Every bubble is the bandwagon with money

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:39.400 align:start position:19%
on it.
Rising prices attract buyers, then the

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:47.640 align:start position:19%
music stops.
Fashion, trends, viral opinions, same

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:52.640 align:start position:19%
mechanism. Popularity hijacks judgment
before it wakes up.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:56.880 align:start position:19%
It bends elections.
Polls pull undecided voters toward the

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.600 align:start position:19%
leader.
People want the winner, not the

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:06.440 align:start position:19%
platform.
Online pile-ons? Nobody reads the

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:10.120 align:start position:19%
original post. They read the outrage and
add their stone.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.080 align:start position:19%
What would you choose if nobody knew
you?

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:17.600 align:start position:19%
If the answer changes, the crowd chose
for you.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:23.480 align:start position:19%
Popularity is not proof. It is peer
pressure wearing a costume.

00:08:20.919 --> 00:08:28.080 align:start position:19%
Trick seven is the quietest. You will
spot it in every interview, date, and

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:36.240 align:start position:19%
courtroom.
Trick seven, the halo effect. One good

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:42.360 align:start position:19%
trait and your brain assumes the rest.
Attractive people get judged kinder and

00:08:38.919 --> 00:08:47.920 align:start position:19%
smarter. Same resume, different face,
different verdict.

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:52.760 align:start position:19%
Celebrities sell perfume and crypto
alike.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:57.920 align:start position:19%
The halo of fame smears onto the
product.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:09:03.240 align:start position:19%
First impressions are halo factories. A
firm handshake and a good suit outweigh

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:09.240 align:start position:19%
an empty resume.
I once hired someone brilliant at

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:14.440 align:start position:19%
discussing the work. Discussion was
their only skill.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:18.320 align:start position:19%
The halo blinded me.
Its evil twin,

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:25.360 align:start position:19%
the horn effect.
One bad trait paints everything dark.

00:09:21.839 --> 00:09:30.720 align:start position:19%
One awkward moment sinks a candidate.
Judge traits separately. Charm gets one

00:09:27.839 --> 00:09:35.400 align:start position:19%
score, competence another. Never average
them.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:41.240 align:start position:19%
Charm is a spotlight. Always check what
is hiding in the shadows.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:49.160 align:start position:19%
Seven tricks, now with names.
Confirmation bias, sunk cost, anchoring,

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:53.760 align:start position:19%
loss aversion, availability, bandwagon,
halo.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:58.320 align:start position:19%
You cannot delete these glitches.
But spot one in the act and it loses its

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:04.160 align:start position:19%
power.
Rewire how you see your own mind.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:08.360 align:start position:19%
Subscribe to Dark Mind Files. Tomorrow,
the seven tricks others use on you.

